WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:02.853
At first glance, a kangaroo's pouch

00:00:02.853 --> 00:00:05.920
looks like nothing but
a built-in baby sling.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.440
But if you peeked inside,

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:11.510
you'd see it's far more
complex than a simple pocket.

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:14.170
It has to be, because the joey inside

00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:16.920
is not your average baby.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.503
An adult male red kangaroo can stand over

00:00:19.503 --> 00:00:23.820
1 1/2 meters tall and weigh 90 kilograms.

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:26.320
That's larger than a grown man.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:29.980
But the newborns start out
the size of jelly beans.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:33.210
They're blind, deaf, and hairless to boot.

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:35.580
After all, they only spend 33 days

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:37.790
inside their mom before birth.

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:39.570
That's like a human having a baby

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:41.890
when she's two months pregnant.

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:44.870
So the underdeveloped
roo isn't ready to face

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:47.640
the harsh Australian wilderness.

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:49.670
That's where the pouch comes in.

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:52.870
It's a pocket of skin that
acts like a second womb,

00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:56.470
giving the joey a safe,
cozy environment to grow.

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:58.160
And, like a pregnant belly,

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:01.860
the pouch can stretch to fit
the baby as it gets bigger.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:03.450
It's lined with powerful,

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:06.240
but flexible, muscles and ligaments.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:09.330
To keep the joey safe, mom
can tighten those muscles

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:11.660
to shut the pouch flush against her body,

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:15.080
just like pulling a drawstring bag closed.

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:17.930
And it will need the extra space,

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:20.030
because over the course of eight months,

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:22.670
that bean-sized baby will reach the size

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:24.750
of a large house cat.

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:27.670
That's thousands of
times its birth weight.

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:31.560
That rapid growth is thanks
to the pouch's four nipples,

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:35.250
which spout milk that contains
germ-fighting antibodies

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:38.070
to keep the little roo from getting sick.

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:39.830
But that's just the start.

00:01:39.830 --> 00:01:41.890
You see, the nutrient levels change

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:45.190
to meet the baby's needs as it ages.

00:01:45.190 --> 00:01:48.620
For example, sulfur, a major
building block of hair,

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:50.700
peaks around three months in.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:54.010
That's the same time the
baby starts growing fur.

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:55.100
The best part?

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:59.340
Mom can produce multiple types
of milk at the same time,

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:02.580
each squirting from its own nipple.

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:04.310
So she can suckle two babies

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:07.860
in different age groups simultaneously.

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:10.743
Another special feature
about the kangaroo pouch

00:02:10.743 --> 00:02:13.090
is that it's lined with sweat glands

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:16.570
that release antimicrobial substances,

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:18.820
which help protect the baby roos

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:22.780
from harmful viruses,
bacteria, and parasites.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:25.330
But there's one more
way that pouch's design

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:27.320
keeps the joey safe.

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:31.620
It's totally hairless, and
that skin-to-skin contact

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:34.050
keeps the baby warm and cozy.

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:37.400
Basically, it's the ultimate nursery.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:39.810
But nothing lasts forever.

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:43.290
Eventually, the joey will
need to leave the pouch.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:46.320
At about 5 months old,
it pokes its head out.

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:48.940
And a month later, it
takes its first tentative

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:50.990
steps into the world.

00:02:50.990 --> 00:02:54.710
There, it will explore
for a few short seconds

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:56.530
before high-tailing it home.

00:02:56.530 --> 00:03:01.260
But as it gets older and
bolder, it stays out longer,

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:02.950
until eight months in:

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:07.533
It's ready to leave the nest,
well, the pouch, for good.

